<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Large Language Models (LLMs) are reshaping today’s business practices, however, their adoption within small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs) raises significant technical, ethical and trust issues. This paper proposes a structured, multi-phased framework designed to embed trust and ethical principles throughout the AI lifecycle for their secure and responsible use in SMEs. Structured around four pillars, i.e., Data, Algorithms, Human oversight, and Model Architecture, the framework bridges theoretical ethical principles with operational practice, enhancing AI capabilities in diverse SME applications. Ultimately, this paper offers a structured roadmap for responsible AI adoption, framing trust and ethics as a catalyst for resilience, competitiveness, and sustainable innovation in SMEs. 
<br class="ltx_break">Keywords: SMEs, Digital transformation, Ethical AI, Trust, Cybersecurity, Data-driven, Human-AI, Sustainable development.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">SMEs are the backbone of national and global economies, representing over 90% of businesses worldwide, around 50% of global employment, and thus playing a significant role in economic sustainability as well as employment generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. In Australia, SMEs represent over 98% of all businesses and contribute nearly one-third of GDP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. Similarly, SMEs dominate European economy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. With the rapid advancement of AI including technologies like machine learning, deep learning and large language models (LLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> (as shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ SME-TEAM: Leveraging Trust and Ethics for Secure and Responsible Use of AI and LLMs in SMEs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) - SMEs are undergoing digital transformation. These emerging technologies enable innovative business models, enhance productivity and customer engagement, intelligent decision-making as well as market expansion with new employment opportunities. Nowadays, the integration of AI technologies and digital transformation is reshaping SME competitiveness across diverse sectors - such as manufacturing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, healthcare <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, agriculture and food <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, finance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> and retail <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. By enhancing efficiency, driving innovation, and enabling adaptive business practices, this transformation positions SMEs as pivotal contributors to growth and resilience in the rapidly evolving digital economy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While AI and digital transformation create immense opportunities, SMEs face a range of challenges and risks that complicate its adoption <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. Technical limitations such as lack of AI model explainability, and vulnerability to errors or adversarial attacks reduce trust in model outcomes. Ethical risks including bias, fairness concerns, and inadequate transparency threaten customer confidence and brand reputation. Operational barriers such as implementation costs and shortages of AI skilled professionals constrain effective deployment and oversight. From a security perspective, digital transformation and AI systems also introduce risks of cyberattacks, data breaches, and adversarial manipulation, which can threaten business continuity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>. Furthermore, weak governance structures, limited regulatory awareness, and absence of clear accountability mechanisms hinder SMEs from embedding responsible AI practices. Collectively, these risks not only endanger customer trust and compliance but also expose SMEs to reputational damage, financial losses, and reduced competitiveness in the evolving digital economy.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.10594v1/AI-LLM-position.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:262/99;" width="262" height="99" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Typically involved technologies such as machine learning (ML), deep learning (DL) and large language models (LLMs) in the broad area of artificial intelligence (AI).</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S1.fig3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:155.3pt;"><img src="2509.10594v1/trade-off.png" id="S1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:182/138;" width="182" height="138" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>An illustration of the key aspects - Trust (T), Ethics (E) and AI Modeling (AM) of SME-TEAM.</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S1.F3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:182.9pt;"><img src="2509.10594v1/SME-TEAM-Scenario.png" id="S1.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:250/138;" width="250" height="138" alt="Refer to caption">
<br class="ltx_break ltx_break">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>A motivational scenario from the perspective of real-world applicability of SME-TEAM.</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To overcome these challenges, SMEs must embed trust and ethical principles at the core of their AI adoption strategies. By prioritizing transparency, explainability, accountability, and secure-by-design approaches in SMEs, technological innovation aligns with societal values and regulatory expectations. Explainable AI mechanisms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> allow stakeholders to understand decision-making processes, strengthening trust among customers, employees, and regulators. Ethical design practices, combined with human oversight, help mitigate bias, ensure equitable outcomes and align AI use with societal values. Importantly, sector-specific tailoring such as bias mitigation in financial services, or transparent consent mechanisms in healthcare, ensures that ethical safeguards align with contextual needs. By adopting secure-by-design architectures and maintaining robust governance strategy, SMEs can balance innovation with responsibility. Ultimately, trust and ethics shift AI adoption from being a potential risk to becoming a strategic driver of competitiveness, resilience, and sustainable innovation in SMEs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Building on these insights, this paper contributes to both theory and practice by addressing the often-overlooked intersection of AI adoption, trust, and ethics within the SME context. <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">First</span>, it advances theoretical understanding by reframing trust and ethics not as constraints but as enablers of competitiveness, resilience, and innovation in digitally transforming SMEs. <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Second</span>, it introduces a structured, multi-phased framework “SME-TEAM", Trust, Ethics and AI Modeling for SMEs (as shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ SME-TEAM: Leveraging Trust and Ethics for Secure and Responsible Use of AI and LLMs in SMEs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) that bridges high-level ethical principles with actionable practices through its four pillars: Data, Algorithms, Human Oversight, and Model Architecture. <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Third</span>, it extends research implications by demonstrating the framework’s potentiality and applicability across SME sectors, encouraging future studies to examine contextual challenges and strategies for sustainable digital transformation. Collectively, these contributions provide both an academic roadmap and practical guidance for SMEs, policymakers, and technology providers seeking to harness AI securely and responsibly.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Digital Transformation and AI in SMEs: Current Practice</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Digital Transformation of SMEs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Digital transformation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> has become a defining feature of the modern SME landscape, enabling smaller enterprises to modernize operations, improve efficiency, and strengthen competitiveness in digital markets. This shift is increasingly driven by the integration of AI into both operational workflows and strategic decision-making, positioning it as a catalyst for SME growth, innovation, and long-term competitiveness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>. Current adoption trends focus on automating repetitive tasks, enhancing customer engagement through chatbots and recommendation systems, and applying data analytics for informed decision-making. For example, Yuwono et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> highlight the use of ICT across diverse SME sectors such as manufacturing, agriculture, and mining; Indiani et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> examine how digital transformation unlocks e-commerce potential; Kohli et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> emphasize its role in strengthening supply chain management; El et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> explore applications in sustainable agriculture; and Vukovic et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> along with Lu et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> focus on financial services. Similarly, Treasure et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> highlight healthcare applications, Graziosi et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> discuss sustainable additive manufacturing, Da et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> analyze manufacturing competitiveness, and De et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> examine the role of digital twin technology in SMEs. While these practices demonstrate the potential of AI and digital technologies to enhance efficiency and competitiveness, they also reveal the uneven readiness of SMEs, largely due to gaps in secure, ethical, and responsible AI adoption.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Revolution of Today’s AI and LLMs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">AI has rapidly progressed from a theoretical concept to a transformative force reshaping industries, business operations, and decision-making. Over the past decade, breakthroughs in machine learning, data analytics, natural language processing, and deep learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> have enabled AI systems to process large datasets, detect intricate patterns, and deliver predictive insights with remarkable accuracy. These advancements not only enhance productivity but also empower organizations to pursue data-driven strategies that boost adaptability and competitiveness. A number of studies, including Wei et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, Arroyabe et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, Carayannis et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, and Mantri et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, underscore the crucial role of AI adoption in improving SME performance, innovation, and long-term competitiveness. Within the wider AI ecosystem, Large Language Models (LLMs) mark a paradigm shift in language-based intelligence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>. Developed on advanced deep learning architectures and trained on vast text corpora, models such as GPT and BERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> exhibit exceptional capabilities in understanding, generating, and reasoning with natural language. Their applications extend across conversational agents, automated content creation, sentiment analysis, summarization, knowledge extraction, and security analytics. For instance, Zhang et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> assess the role of LLMs in phishing detection for SMEs, Carayannis et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> highlight their potential in enhancing SME resilience and competitiveness, and Kopka et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> investigate their broader impact on productivity and innovation in SMEs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While AI technologies hold significant promise, SMEs often struggle to fully leverage them due to persistent concerns around trust and ethical implications <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. In the Australian context <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, adoption rates vary markedly by enterprise size: 82% among businesses with 200–500 employees, 68% for those with 20–199 employees, 40% for firms with 5–19 employees, and only 33% for micro-enterprises with 0–4 employees. Sectoral differences are also evident, with relatively higher adoption in retail trade (46%), health and education (45%), services (43%), and hospitality (42%), compared to lower uptake in distribution (31%), construction (30%), manufacturing (28%), and agriculture, forestry, and fishing (19%). Despite these disparities, AI remains particularly transformative for SMEs, and to fully realise its benefits, it is essential to not only recognise but also proactively address the associated risks, as discussed in the following sections.</p>
</div>
