</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>AI Risk Factors and Security Challenges</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The rapid integration of AI and LLMs into business operations introduces a complex array of risk factors and cybersecurity challenges <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> that SMEs must address to ensure secure and responsible adoption. AI systems are highly susceptible to adversarial attacks, where carefully manipulated inputs distort outputs and lead to flawed or harmful decisions. Threats such as data poisoning and model inversion attacks compromise the integrity and confidentiality of training datasets, while risks specific to LLMs, such as hallucinations and prompt injection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, increase exposure to misinformation, manipulation, and unreliable outcomes. Beyond these technical vulnerabilities, AI adoption magnifies broader challenges including algorithmic bias, opacity in decision-making, and over-reliance on automation, all of which can erode accountability and stakeholder trust. SMEs with their comparatively limited cybersecurity resources and governance mechanisms, are particularly vulnerable. Still many SMEs are not ready to adopt AI due to these risk factors and ethical issues <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>. This underscores the crucial importance of embedding secure-by-design principles, robust data governance, continuous monitoring, and structured human oversight across the AI lifecycle.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The identified gaps can be broadly classified into four domains: technical gaps, ethical gaps, operational gaps, and human–AI integration gaps, as summarised in Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 2.2 Revolution of Today’s AI and LLMs ‣ 2 Digital Transformation and AI in SMEs: Current Practice ‣ SME-TEAM: Leveraging Trust and Ethics for Secure and Responsible Use of AI and LLMs in SMEs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Addressing these gaps is essential to ensure that SMEs can adopt AI and LLMs in ways that are not only innovative and efficient but also secure, ethical, and trustworthy. The multi-phased framework proposed in this paper is specifically designed to bridge these gaps, providing SMEs with actionable guidance across the pre-modeling, in-modeling, and post-modeling stages of the AI lifecycle.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Conceptual Foundation for the Framework</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Trust and Ethical AI Principles</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The adoption of AI in SMEs must be guided by a set of core trust and ethical principles that serve as both a moral foundation and an operational compass. Drawing on existing studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>, we outline the following key principles, which are interlinked and mutually reinforcing, to ensure that AI adoption promotes secure, responsible, and sustainable outcomes. These are: <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fairness and non-discrimination</span> ensuring AI systems avoid bias and treat all groups equitably; <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Accountability and responsibility</span> which require clear mechanisms of oversight, liability, and redress for AI-driven outcomes; <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Transparency and explainability</span> which make decision pathways interpretable for both technical and non-technical stakeholders; <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Privacy and data protection</span> safeguard personal information and ensure compliance with regulatory standards; <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Safety and security</span> embedding resilience against adversarial attacks, misuse, and system vulnerabilities; <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Human-centredness and oversight</span> emphasise the necessity of retaining meaningful human control, especially in high-stakes contexts; <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Inclusivity and accessibility</span> ensure that AI serves diverse users and helps bridge digital divides; <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Trustworthiness and reliability</span> highlight the importance of delivering consistent, dependable results that inspire confidence; <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Contestability</span> guarantees that individuals, groups, or communities impacted by AI systems have timely avenues to challenge outcomes or decisions; and finally, <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Human, societal, and environmental wellbeing</span> reinforces the principle that AI adoption should advance not only business efficiency but also the broader interests of society and the environment. Together, these principles provide SMEs with actionable guidance to integrate AI and LLMs responsibly, balancing innovation with accountability and ensuring that digital transformation contributes to long-term resilience and sustainable growth.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Framework Pillars: Mapping the Foundational Principles</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The secure and responsible adoption of AI and LLMs in SMEs is grounded in four interdependent pillars - Data, Algorithms, Human, and Model Architecture, which collectively translate the principles of trust and ethics into actionable practices across the AI lifecycle in diverse SME contexts, as shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.2 Framework Pillars: Mapping the Foundational Principles ‣ 3 Conceptual Foundation for the Framework ‣ SME-TEAM: Leveraging Trust and Ethics for Secure and Responsible Use of AI and LLMs in SMEs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. These pillars serve as the structural foundation for translating abstract values into actionable practices, enabling SMEs to effectively harness the potential of AI technologies, as discussed below.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.10594v1/SME-TEAM-pillars.png" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:286/98;" width="286" height="98" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>An illustration of SME-TEAM pillars with different application areas.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Data -</span> is the foundational pillar of adopting AI technologies, as the reliability of AI outputs depends directly on the quality, authenticity, and provenance of inputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>. For SMEs, which often rely on internal data, e.g., sales records, customer feedback, supply chains; external data, e.g., industry reports, market trends, social media, economic indicators; and real-time feeds, e.g., IoT sensors, sentiment analysis, web analytics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, ensuring data integrity through bias detection, anonymisation, and compliance with privacy regulations is crucial. By embedding mechanisms for provenance tracking and validation, SMEs can safeguard against vulnerabilities such as poisoning, hidden skewness, and regulatory breaches, thereby building confidence in downstream decision-making.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Algorithms -</span> represent the computational engines that transform data into actionable insights. Their design must prioritise fairness, robustness, and resilience to adversarial manipulation. For SMEs, this means selecting or developing algorithms such as machine learning, deep learning, data and knowledge mining, language modeling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> that balance efficiency with accountability, ensuring that outputs are explainable and free from algorithmic bias. Embedding ethical checks within algorithmic processes allows SMEs to prevent discriminatory outcomes, making algorithmic accountability central to trustworthy AI.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Human oversight -</span> is also important and serves as the ethical anchor of the framework. While AI can provide automation and scale, SMEs cannot afford to relinquish responsibility to opaque systems. Human-in-the-loop and human-on-the-loop mechanisms ensure that contextual awareness, domain expertise, and ethical reflection remain central to high-stakes decision-making. This pillar emphasises that accountability must always be shared, with clear escalation pathways where human judgement prevails over automated recommendations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Model Architecture -</span> provides the technical scaffolding that binds the other pillars together. Secure-by-design principles ensure resilience against cyber threats and adversarial attacks, while explainability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> mechanisms create transparent pathways for non-technical validation of outcomes. Context-aware architectures further ensure that model outputs are aligned with SME-specific objectives, sectoral requirements, and ethical norms. By embedding these safeguards within system design, SMEs can achieve both operational efficiency and ethical reliability.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Framework Pillars: Mapping Foundational Principles for SMEs</figcaption>
