<span id="S3.T2.2.5.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">Provides technical scaffolding. Secure-by-design principles enhance resilience, transparency and explainability supports non-technical validation and context-aware architectures align outputs with SME objectives, sectoral norms, and ethical standards.</span>
</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Taken together, these four pillars map the foundational principles of trust, ethics, and secure AI modeling into actionable practices, as summarized in Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 3.2 Framework Pillars: Mapping the Foundational Principles ‣ 3 Conceptual Foundation for the Framework ‣ SME-TEAM: Leveraging Trust and Ethics for Secure and Responsible Use of AI and LLMs in SMEs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. They offer SMEs a coherent structure for embedding resilience, accountability, and transparency across diverse application areas, ensuring that AI adoption is not only technologically effective but also socially legitimate and sustainable.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>The Framework: SME-TEAM</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To enable secure and responsible adoption of AI in SMEs, this paper introduces a structured, multi-phased framework “SME-TEAM" consisting of seven layers, grounded in trust, ethics, and AI modeling, as shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4 The Framework: SME-TEAM ‣ SME-TEAM: Leveraging Trust and Ethics for Secure and Responsible Use of AI and LLMs in SMEs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. The framework provides SMEs with a structured, scalable roadmap that integrates trust and ethical principles across the AI lifecycle. It is designed to be adaptable, offering SMEs guidance that balances innovation with risk mitigation, ensuring systems are both operationally effective and socially responsible. In the following, we discuss the components of the framework, structured across three phases.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.10594v1/SME-TEAM-framework.png" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:333/401;" width="333" height="401" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>An illustration of SME-TEAM framework.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Pre-Modelling Phase:</span> The pre-modelling phase establishes the foundation for secure and responsible AI adoption in SMEs by embedding governance, ethics, and data stewardship before any technical integration begins. In contexts where resources are limited but risks can be disproportionately high, SMEs must first develop ethical and trust foundations through lightweight but explicit guidelines that emphasize fairness, inclusivity, accountability, and transparency, ensuring alignment with sectoral expectations and regulatory requirements. At the same time, ensuring data integrity and provenance becomes essential, with mechanisms such as metadata tagging, bias detection, anonymisation, and privacy compliance safeguarding the authenticity and reliability of input datasets. By addressing vulnerabilities such as data poisoning, manipulation, and hidden skewness early, this phase creates a credible baseline of trust and integrity. Collectively, the pre-modelling practices ensure that SMEs approach AI adoption with the necessary ethical, legal, and contextual readiness, avoiding premature experimentation and fostering the resilience required for trustworthy AI systems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In-Modelling Phase:</span> The in-modelling phase forms the technical core of AI adoption within the knowledge value chain <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>, embedding resilience, transparency, and contextual alignment into model design and deployment. Secure-by-design practices strengthen robustness against adversarial threats, anomaly detection failures, and injection attacks, thereby safeguarding SMEs from vulnerabilities that could undermine reliability. Simultaneously, explainability and interpretability mechanisms address the “black-box” challenge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> of AI and LLMs by offering human-centred transparency through decision pathways, counterfactual reasoning, and intuitive visualisation dashboards that enable non-technical stakeholders to validate results. In parallel, context-aware alignment integrates ethical and sector-specific guardrails, ensuring outputs remain responsible, compliant, and relevant across sensitive domains such as finance, healthcare, and manufacturing, while minimising unintended consequences. Collectively, these measures transform modelling from a purely technical exercise into a secure, interpretable, and ethically aligned process that strengthens trust and operational credibility for SMEs adopting AI.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Post-Modelling Phase:</span> The post-modelling phase secures the long-term trust, accountability, and adaptability of AI systems in SMEs by embedding continuous governance and oversight into day-to-day operations. Human–AI collaboration plays a central role, with decision-making protocols formalising human-in-the-loop mechanisms that balance automation with expert judgement, ensuring clear escalation pathways in high-risk domains such as finance, healthcare, and safety-critical industries. Alongside this, continuous monitoring and ethical auditing introduce adaptive safeguards, including bias re-evaluation, drift detection, compliance checks, and performance tracking, enabling SMEs to adjust dynamically to shifting ethical standards, regulatory requirements, and operational environments. Collectively, these practices shift deployment from a static endpoint to a proactive, ongoing lifecycle, ensuring that AI systems remain transparent, accountable, and trustworthy well beyond their initial rollout.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Together, these seven layers form a holistic, phased framework for AI adoption in SMEs. By embedding trust, ethics, and AI modeling across the entire lifecycle, the framework equips SMEs to manage operational and ethical challenges while harnessing AI as a catalyst for innovation, competitiveness, and sustainable growth.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Realizing Potential and Applicability in SMEs</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section explores how the framework drives SME economic growth through its applicability and potential, while also fostering innovative and sustainable business practices. Key points include:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Business Model and Digital Value Creation:</span>
Business models in the digital era increasingly rely on data-driven strategies and AI-enabled capabilities to create and capture value <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. Digital value creation transforms raw data, customer interactions, and operational processes into innovative products, services, and experiences that enhance competitiveness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>. For SMEs, integrating AI into their business models enables new revenue streams, personalised offerings, and greater scalability. By embedding trust, transparency, and ethical principles, digital value creation ensures not only economic growth but also socially responsible and sustainable business practices.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Optimization and Increased Productivity and Services:</span>
Optimization and increased productivity in SMEs can be achieved by leveraging AI to streamline operations, automate repetitive tasks, and enhance decision-making through data-driven insights <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. Predictive analytics, intelligent resource allocation, and process automation enable businesses to achieve more with fewer resources, a critical advantage where budgets and staff are limited. By embedding secure, explainable, and context-aware AI systems, SMEs can enhance productivity while ensuring high-quality service without compromising reliability or trust, reducing costs, and sustaining competitiveness in dynamic digital markets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Enhancing Customer Satisfaction and Engagement:</span>
Enhancing customer satisfaction and engagement requires SMEs to harness AI for personalised, responsive, and trust-driven interactions. By using data-driven insights <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, businesses can anticipate customer needs, tailor recommendations, and deliver seamless support through tools like chatbots and virtual assistants. Explainability and transparency in AI decisions build confidence, while ethical safeguards ensure fairness and privacy. This responsible approach not only improves satisfaction but also deepens engagement, turning customers into loyal advocates and positioning SMEs competitively in the digital marketplace.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Risk management, Cybersecurity and Resilience:</span>
Risk management, cybersecurity and resilience are essential for SMEs to operate safely in an increasingly digital ecosystem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>. Embedding secure-by-design architectures, continuous monitoring, and explainable decision-making allows businesses to identify vulnerabilities early and respond proactively to emerging threats. AI-powered analytics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> enhance anomaly detection, fraud prevention, and predictive risk assessment, while human oversight ensures accountability in high-stakes decisions. By protecting data, maintaining regulatory compliance, and reinforcing resilience, SMEs can safeguard business continuity, strengthen stakeholder trust, and sustain secure operations in a rapidly evolving cyber landscape.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Business Reputation Enhancement:</span>
Business reputation enhancement is vital for SMEs to establish trust, credibility, and long-term success in competitive markets. By adopting ethical AI practices, ensuring transparency, and safeguarding customer data, SMEs can project reliability and integrity. A strong reputation not only attracts customers and investors but also strengthens partnerships and regulatory confidence. Consistently delivering secure, fair, and high-quality services enhances brand image and customer loyalty. Ultimately, reputation becomes a strategic asset, enabling SMEs to achieve resilience, growth, and sustainable competitive advantage.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Market Expansion and Creating New Employment Opportunities:</span>
Market expansion and employment creation are key outcomes of AI adoption in SMEs. By leveraging data-driven insights, predictive analytics, and intelligent decision-making, SMEs can identify new opportunities, personalise offerings, and expand into domestic and international markets. AI-enabled scalability lowers entry barriers, enhances competitiveness, and supports innovative business models. As SMEs grow, they create new employment opportunities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>. Embedding ethical and responsible AI practices ensures this growth is sustainable, inclusive, and aligned with long-term societal and economic goals.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p8.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, by aligning productivity gains with customer trust, risk reduction, and sustainable innovation, it provides a balanced pathway for SMEs to harness AI securely while maintaining resilience and competitiveness in the evolving digital economy.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Implications for Research</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The framework highlights the importance of developing AI technologies that are not only technically robust but also contextually practical for SMEs, which often operate with constrained budgets, limited computational resources, and smaller technical teams compared to large enterprises. Many existing approaches to explainability, bias detection, and adversarial robustness are tailored to enterprise-scale environments, requiring significant infrastructure and expertise that SMEs cannot realistically sustain. Addressing this gap demands research into lightweight, resource-efficient approaches, such as simplified explainability tools that produce human-readable insights, bias detection algorithms tailored to SME datasets, and robustness mechanisms capable of functioning on standard SME-level infrastructure. By adapting innovations to the SME context, barriers to adoption can be reduced, ensuring that responsible and trustworthy AI becomes accessible beyond large corporations and contributes broadly to the economic ecosystem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond efficiency, there is an urgent need to develop sector-specific interpretability frameworks that translate AI outputs into insights meaningful within their application context. While general-purpose interpretability methods offer algorithmic transparency, they often fail to address the ethical and operational realities of specific domains. In finance, this requires risk models to be explainable in terms of fairness and compliance with lending regulations; in healthcare, interpretability must align with clinical protocols and patient safety standards; in manufacturing, explanations should emphasise implications for quality assurance and operational efficiency. Advancing research into domain-specific interpretability ensures that AI outputs are not only transparent but also actionable and ethically grounded, enabling SME stakeholders to adopt AI-driven decision-making with greater confidence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Another promising avenue is the development of adaptive auditing and drift detection methods tailored to SME environments. Unlike static compliance checks, adaptive auditing supports continuous monitoring, enabling SMEs to stay aligned with evolving ethical standards and regulatory requirements without relying on constant external oversight. Research can advance this by designing lightweight systems that detect data drift, concept drift, and ethical risks in real time, allowing vulnerabilities to be addressed before they escalate into systemic failures. Beyond the technical dimension, interdisciplinary collaboration is equally vital. Insights from social sciences, business studies, and ethics can help uncover how organisational culture, employee trust, and stakeholder values influence AI adoption, ensuring that technological innovations evolve into socially responsible solutions that reflect the practical realities of SMEs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Discussion and Final Remarks</h2>

<section id="S7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.1 </span>Theoretical Contributions</h3>

<div id="S7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper contributes to the expanding body of research on secure and responsible AI by proposing a structured framework that embeds trust and ethical principles throughout the AI lifecycle in the SME context. The framework advances theory in four key ways. First, it bridges the gap between abstract ethical principles and their operationalisation, illustrating how values such as fairness, transparency, and accountability can be translated into actionable layers of AI adoption. Second, it introduces four conceptual pillars - Data, Algorithms, Human Oversight, and Model Architecture, as theoretical anchors that connect ethical values with practical implementation, offering a robust lens for balancing technical, organisational, and ethical dimensions. Third, it adopts a multi-phased perspective encompassing pre-modelling, in-modelling, and post-modelling stages, thereby providing a holistic understanding of ethical AI deployment across the full pipeline rather than in isolation. Finally, it extends AI governance debates by positioning trust and ethics not as constraints but as enablers of sustainable innovation, resilience, and competitiveness. Collectively, these contributions establish a novel foundation for understanding how SMEs can integrate AI responsibly while ensuring long-term growth and societal value.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.2 </span>Practical Implications</h3>

<div id="S7.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond its theoretical significance, the framework provides SMEs with a practical roadmap for adopting AI securely and responsibly. It enables organisations to leverage automation, predictive insights, and personalisation while maintaining data integrity, protecting privacy, and ensuring regulatory compliance. For business leaders, it offers actionable guidance on enhancing productivity, expanding market opportunities, mitigating cyber risks, and driving sustainable innovation. Crucially, it emphasises digital market expansion alongside new employment opportunities, ensuring that transformation supports workforce development rather than displacement. For policymakers, the framework serves as a scalable blueprint for shaping SME-focused digital strategies, funding schemes, and regulatory interventions. Collectively, it illustrates how trust and ethics centred AI adoption can position SMEs as resilient, innovative, and competitive actors in the digital economy.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
